        "at": "2026-10-18T22:42:11"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "PLATEAU",
      "narration_text": "The first reason is the surface itself. SERS mostly sees molecules sitting on the metal, and the surface only has so many places to sit. At low concentrations, signal climbs quickly. As the sites fill, it levels off, following the shape chemists call a Langmuir isotherm. Past that point, more analyte barely changes the signal.",
      "new_visual_element": "Langmuir plateau",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 0.8,
            "event": "axes + surface sites"
          },
          {
            "at": 3.4,
            "event": "curve climbs; sites fill"
          },
          {
            "at": 10.4,
            "event": "plateau; sites full"
          },
          {
            "at": 14.1,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B02_SurfaceFills",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The surface fills up",
          "mechanic": "Calibration curve grows in three segments while eight surface sites fill",
          "note": "ILLUSTRATIVE Langmuir-type curve. Saturation of SERS signal with surface coverage: e.g. arXiv:2412.13882 (quasi-plateau ≥1e-5 M, consistent with Langmuir)."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.64,
      "render_duration_s": 17.666666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:42:11"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "LOTTERY",
      "narration_text": "The second reason you've already met: hot spots. The same sample, measured at three spots, can give three different intensities, because each spot samples a different set of hot spots. So raw intensity alone is a noisy ruler.",
      "new_visual_element": "Spot-to-spot intensity",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 1.3,
            "event": "three spots, three intensities"
          },
          {
            "at": 5.4,
            "event": "'noisy ruler' caption"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.9,
            "event": "bars re-measure differently"
          },
          {
            "at": 10.4,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B03_HotSpotLottery",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The hot-spot lottery",
          "mechanic": "Three bars of one sample change height on re-measurement",
          "note": "ILLUSTRATIVE heights. Mechanism: hot-spot enhancement distribution (see sers-hot-spots reel; Fang et al. 2008)."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.29,
      "render_duration_s": 12.291666666666666,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:42:11"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "INTERNAL_STANDARD",
      "narration_text": "The common fix is an internal standard: a reference molecule that shares the same surface. If a spot is hotter, both signals rise together, so the ratio stays steadier than either signal alone. It isn't free. The standard can compete with the analyte for surface sites, overlap its peaks, or degrade under the laser.",
      "new_visual_element": "Ratio to a reference",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "lane": "manim",
        "show": [
          {
            "at": 0.0,
            "event": "title + footer"
          },
          {
            "at": 1.3,
            "event": "analyte + standard bars per spot"
          },
          {
            "at": 7.7,
            "event": "ratio bars, equal"
          },
          {
            "at": 11.6,
            "event": "caveats caption"
          },
          {
            "at": 15.7,
            "event": "kicker"
          }
        ]
      },
      "graphic": {
        "engine": "manim",
        "manim": "B04_InternalStandard",
        "production_viz": {
          "label": "The internal standard",
          "mechanic": "Paired bars scale together; ratio bars are level",
          "note": "IDEALIZED (ratios drawn equal). Caveats from the NIST-affiliated study (J. Phys. Chem. Lett. 2020, doi:10.1021/acs.jpclett.0c03056): site competition, spectral interference, photodegradation."
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.79,
      "render_duration_s": 16.791666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-10-18T22:42:11"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "VERDICT",
      "narration_text": "Here's the verdict. SERS signal saturates as the surface fills, and it swings with the hot spots under your laser. Internal standards tame the second problem, at a cost. And because results still differ between labs, careful calibration and shared good practice matter as much as raw sensitivity.",
      "new_visual_element": "Verdict card",
      "shot": {
        "type": "REMOTION",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeVerdictArtifact",
          "props": {
            "artifactTitle": "SERS Quantitation · Summary",
            "artifactHeading": "Sensitive is not the same as quantitative",
            "artifactLines": [
              "Signal saturates as surface sites fill (Langmuir-type behaviour).",
              "Calibrate within the range where the response is still rising.",
              "Hot spots make raw intensity vary from spot to spot.",
              "Internal standard: ratio to a reference molecule on the same surface.",
              "Caveats: surface competition, peak overlap, photodegradation.",
              "Results still differ between labs; good analytical practice matters."
            ]
          },
          "rendered": {
